contents. It is located at the beginning of the article, before the first heading. It is not a news-style lead or 
"lede" paragraph.
4. An article's content should begin with an introductory lead section – a concise summary of the article – which is 
never divided into sections. The remainder of the article is typically divided into sections.
5. The lead section gives the basics in a nutshell, introduces the article, and cultivates interest in reading 
on—though not by teasing the reader or hinting at what follows.
6. The lead section should be written in a clear, accessible style with a neutral point of view.
7. The lead should stand on its own as a concise overview of the article's topic. It should identify the topic, 
establish context, explain why the topic is notable, and summarize the most important points, including any prominent
controversies. The notability of the article's subject is usually established in the first few sentences.
8. As in the body of the article itself, the emphasis given to material in the lead should roughly reflect its 
importance to the topic, according to reliable, published sources. Apart from basic facts, significant information 
