ring first appeared in scholarly literature in 1925, after Arthur Evans privately purchased it and published it in 
the Journal of Hellenic Studies in an article titled 'A glimpse into the Minoan After-World'. Evans interpreted the 
bezel scene as a human couple journeying to and entering a Minoan Elysian domain or 'Elysian Fields'; he read the 
dominant cross-shaped motif as a 'Tree of Life' comparable to Oriental and Scandinavian mythology, butterflies and 
chrysalises as symbols of the human soul, and a lion as guardian of the gate to the After-World. Recent scholarship 
(Hudler, 'E-scaping arguments') situates the Ring of Nestor within debates on gemmae dubitandae and a relational 
archaeology approach, arguing that such sealstones and signet rings, which lack decisive provenance evidence, must 
indefinitely be treated as both fake and authentic archaeological evidence (likened to 'Schroedinger’s cat'), that 
the technical term gemma dubitanda is criticized yet indispensable for grouping these objects and articulating the 
dilemma of their uncertain status, and that forgers, archaeologists, collectors, and other consumers of antiquities 
are mutually dependent actors in a shared social and scholarly environment. The same study embeds these discussions 
in the history of Aegean Bronze Age archaeology, emphasizing the late 19th–early 20th century growth in demand for 
Aegean artifacts and related forgeries, and the long-lasting influence of pioneers like Arthur Evans, whose 
terminological and chronological frameworks dominated the field until they were seriously challenged from the 1970s 
