acquisition and 1925 publication by Arthur Evans, Evans's eschatological interpretation, later debates on 
authenticity and dating (16th\x13\x150th century BC), and its role as a paradigmatic gemma dubitanda in discussions 
of Aegean forgeries and relational archaeology.\\n2. Description: Physical characteristics (large gold signet ring, 
Creto\x13\x150th century BC gold rings), general dimensions and style as evidenced in CMS and Eliopoulos; overview of
the bezel scene (world tree, human figures, animal or scorpion, butterflies/chrysalises) and its compositional 
features.\\n3. Alleged discovery and provenance: Narrative of reported find in a tholos tomb at Kakovatos near Pylos;
lack of professional excavation documentation; examination by the Athens archaeological board, rejection as a modern 
forgery, identification of Georg Karo; subsequent private acquisition by Arthur Evans; museum or collection history 
as far as documented in the sources.\\n4. Dating: Placement within Creto\x13\x15th century BC gold rings and Late 
Bronze Age Aegean material; discussion of stylistic and technical grounds for dating; mention of alternative views or
earlier skepticism about authenticity that affected chronological assessments.\\n5. Evans's interpretation: Summary 
of Evans's 1925 JHS article 'A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World' \x13\x150 interpretation of the scene as a 
journey to a Minoan afterlife or Elysian Fields, with Tree of Life, soul-symbol butterflies, and lion/dragon 
guardian; comparison with cross-cultural ('Oriental' and Scandinavian) analogies he invoked.\\n6. Later scholarship 
and alternative interpretations: Coverage of Eliopoulos's reinterpretation of the lion/dragon as a scorpion, its 
apotropaic function, and implications for understanding the scene; mention of other suggested readings (female deity,
fertility, seasonal/cyclical motifs, eternity) and the acknowledged limits of current knowledge about Middle Helladic
and LH II contexts.\\n7. Authenticity debates and gemma dubitanda framework: Discussion of early suspicions and the 
Athens board's forgery judgment; subsequent reassessments of authenticity (including Eliopoulos's arguments and new 
comparanda); Hudler's framing of the Ring of Nestor as a gemma dubitanda within relational archaeology, treating such
objects as simultaneously possibly genuine and fake; the conceptual and historiographical implications for Aegean 
Bronze Age archaeology, including the history of forgeries and the dominance and later critique of Arthur Evans's 
frameworks.\\n8. Reception and significance: The ring's place in modern scholarship on Aegean iconography, religion, 
