"20260719003545052907_dfc74d21", "type": "Analysis", "analysis_type": "insight", "text": "Current evidence 
establishes that the Ring of Nestor is an alleged Aegean Bronze Age gold signet ring, reportedly found in a tholos 
tomb at Kakovatos near Pylos, but lacking secure excavation provenance. The ring was examined by members of the 
Athens archaeological board, who declined to purchase it on the grounds that they considered it a modern forgery; the
only securely identified member involved was Georg Karo. Arthur Evans later acquired the ring privately and first 
published it in 1925 in the Journal of Hellenic Studies in an article titled \"A glimpse into the Minoan 
After-World.\" Evans interpreted the bezel scene as showing a human couple entering a Minoan afterlife realm or 
\"Elysian Fields,\" with a cross-shaped structure as a \"Tree of Life,\" butterflies and chrysalises as soul symbols,
and a lion as guardian of the afterworld gate. Recent scholarship by Hudler treats the Ring of Nestor as a classic 
gemma dubitanda, emphasizing that its authenticity cannot be decisively confirmed or disproved and arguing that such 
objects must be regarded as both potentially genuine and potentially fake. This scholarship situates the ring within 
broader debates about forgery, relational archaeology, and the historiography of Aegean Bronze Age studies."}, {"id":
"20260719003339400148_13446397", "type": "Statement", "confidence": "high", "qualifiers": [], "source_id": 
"20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "text": "作者强调，对某一印章或戒指归入“gemma 
dubitanda”的有效性取决于研究者是否严格遵守相关参数，并清晰给出论证理由与支持或反对的证据。", "statement_key": 
