"source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003339778859_854e4393", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"“In this paper, gemma dubitanda is regarded a technical term that, in its plural form, specifies a group of 
sealstones and signet rings of alleged historical origin and significance supposedly dating back to the Aegean Bronze
Age… Since none of them originate from professional, well-documented archaeological excavations, doubts about their 
affiliation with Bronze Age material are appropriate or even imperative.”", "source_ids": 
["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003339049197_43b4b972", 
"title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003339792250_c7308daf", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“Nonetheless, 
to this day we do not possess any decisive reference of their provenance, which is why they indefinitely must be 
considered as both, as fake and as authentic archaeological evidence. Taking into account this dual quality, we might
paraphrase them as a ‘Schroedinger’s cat’ of Aegean Archaeology, genuine and fake at the same time.”", "source_ids": 
["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003339049197_43b4b972", 
"title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003339807003_997f1017", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“The 
expression gemma dubitanda has also been accused of being an artificial, unjustified category to benefit those too 
convenient or undecided to take a stand for genuine or fake in their evaluation of objects lacking a traceable place 
of origin. However, in my opinion, not only does its concise terminology facilitate grouping these objects, but, as 
for today, the phrase is indispensable for putting the following crucial dilemma into words…”", "source_ids": 
["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003339049197_43b4b972", 
"title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003339819996_d1cb9f82", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“Minoan and 
Mycenaean forgeries more and more developed to be a substantial problem to Aegean Archaeology in the late 19th and 
early 20th century… From this point on, one can observe a growing demand for Aegean Bronze Age artifacts, sealstones 
and signet rings represented prominently amongst other classes of evidence. This stimulated, as a crucial by-product,
the production of fake artifacts.”", "source_ids": ["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": 
[{"source_id": "20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003339834014_d09b0f88", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“By 
incorporating the expressions scaping (a neologism derived from the conference title ‘No (e)scape?’) and escaping in 
the title of my paper, I aim at highlighting that gemmae dubitandae can be an integral part of relational 
archaeology… The made-up verb scaping… implies the fashioning of a pattern, in this case the result of year-long 
discussions on one joint research issue.”", "source_ids": ["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], 
"sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003339846960_ac7922ce", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“Allegedly 
discovered in a tholos tomb in Kakovatos near Pylos, the gold signet ring commonly known as the ‘Ring of Nestor’… 
made its first appearance in scholarly literature in 1925. By then, Arthur Evans had privately purchased the object 
and published it in the ‘Journal of Hellenic Studies,’ even though the members of the archaeological board at Athens 
commissioned with its examination had refused to buy it from its owner beforehand because they considered it a 
forgery manufactured by a modern artist. The only name that has been determined in the context of the archaeological 
board so far is Georg Karo's.”", "source_ids": ["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": 
[{"source_id": "20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003339860401_c551e829", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“Arthur Evans 
first published the ‘Ring of Nestor’ in an article called ‘A glimpse into the Minoan After-World’ and interpreted the
pictorial scenery on its bezel as illustrative of a Minoan Elysian domain. He identified a human couple on its 
journey to the Beyond and entering the Elysian Fields.”", "source_ids": ["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], 
"citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003339873123_bbc34848", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“In his eyes, 
the dominant, cross-shaped structure resembled the ‘Tree of Life’, known from Oriental and Scandinavian mythology, 
the butterflies and chrysalises… could be explained as symbols of the human soul… a lion… guarded the After-World’s 
gate…”", "source_ids": ["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003339886325_80f8feb4", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“Having been 
characterized as the most influential period of scholarship so far, the earliest years of Aegean Bronze Age 
Archaeology attract an especially high level of attention… For decades it persisted and continued to be determined by
the terminological corset and chronological benchmarks specified by Arthur Evans and other pioneers of Cretan 
archaeology.”", "source_ids": ["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003339900859_2a31be43", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“Although from
the 1970s onwards the school of New Archaeology introduced and established anthropological methods as an alternative 
explanatory scale, its concepts regarding Aegean Bronze Age civilizations still were based on evolutionism. Yiannis 
Hamilakis therefore classified this as ‘cultural neo-evolutionism’…”", "source_ids": 
["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003339049197_43b4b972", 
"title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003432385363_0399b9c3", "type": "Claim", "text": "The gold signet 
ring commonly known as the ‘Ring of Nestor’ is alleged to have been discovered in a tholos tomb at Kakovatos near 
Pylos and first appeared in scholarly literature in 1925, after Arthur Evans privately purchased and published it.", 
"source_ids": ["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003432440751_d1604585", "type": "Claim", "text": "Members of the 
archaeological board at Athens examined the so‑called ‘Ring of Nestor’ before Arthur Evans acquired it, refused to 
purchase it because they considered it a modern forgery, and the only board member securely identified so far is 
Georg Karo.", "source_ids": ["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003432455100_1da27dbd", "type": "Claim", "text": "Arthur Evans 
first published the ‘Ring of Nestor’ in the Journal of Hellenic Studies in an article titled ‘A Glimpse into the 
Minoan After‑World’, interpreting the bezel scene as a human couple entering a Minoan Elysian domain or ‘Elysian 
Fields’.", "source_ids": ["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003432476146_0b140215", "type": "Claim", "text": "In Arthur Evans’s
iconographic interpretation of the ‘Ring of Nestor’, the cross‑shaped central motif was seen as a ‘Tree of Life’ 
comparable to Oriental and Scandinavian myth, the butterflies and chrysalises as symbols of the human soul, and a 
lion as guardian of the gate to the After‑World.", "source_ids": ["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2],
"sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003432499940_55f63eb7", "type": "Claim", "text": "The ‘Ring of 
Nestor’ is treated in recent scholarship as a key example of a gemma dubitanda: a sealstone or signet ring of alleged
Aegean Bronze Age date and significance that lacks secure, professionally documented archaeological provenance, so 
that it must be regarded as possibly genuine and possibly fake at the same time.", "source_ids": 
["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003339049197_43b4b972", 
"title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003432539055_735a895c", "type": "Claim", "text": "The expression 
gemma dubitanda has been criticized as an artificial, unjustified category that allows scholars to avoid taking a 
firm position on authenticity, but the author of the current study argues that the term is nevertheless indispensable
for grouping such objects and articulating the dilemma created by their uncertain status.", "source_ids": 
["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003339049197_43b4b972", 
"title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003432560139_415e7a25", "type": "Claim", "text": "The same study 
emphasizes that new archaeological finds often prompt re‑evaluation of gemmae dubitandae like the ‘Ring of Nestor’, 
but that expecting new material alone to provide a definitive key to their authenticity is unrealistic.", 
"source_ids": ["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003432586862_8eefea91", "type": "Claim", "text": "The study 
situates debates over the ‘Ring of Nestor’ and similar objects within a broader history of Aegean Bronze Age 
archaeology in which early pioneers such as Arthur Evans imposed powerful terminological schemes and evolutionary 
chronological frameworks that shaped the field for decades and were only seriously challenged from the 1970s 
onward.", "source_ids": ["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003339049197_43b4b972", "title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003432629808_a413abe6", "type": "Claim", "text": "The paper on the 
‘Ring of Nestor’ frames gemmae dubitandae within a relational archaeology approach, arguing that forgers, 
archaeologists, collectors, and other consumers of antiquities should be viewed as mutually dependent actors within a
shared social and scholarly environment, and introduces the notions of ‘scaping’ and ‘escaping’ to describe how 
long‑term debates both shape and selectively discard lines of argument about authenticity.", "source_ids": 
["20260719003339049197_43b4b972"], "citations": [2], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003339049197_43b4b972", 
"title": "[PDF] No (e)scape?", "url": 
"https://archiv.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/propylaeumdok/4408/1/Hudler_E-scaping%20arguments_2019.pdf", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003432664536_6c7c5622", "type": "Claim", "text": "A separate study 
by Kyriakos Papathanassiou provides an in‑depth analysis of the Mycenaean signet ring CMS I, 179 from the Tiryns 
Treasure, describing its physical characteristics and offering iconographic interpretations that link its engravings 
to deities, fertility, seasonal cycles, and broader spiritual or cultural significance, though these interpretations 
rely on limited archaeological and mythological context for the LH II and MH periods.", "source_ids": 
["20260719003338997020_f08e518f"], "citations": [1], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003338997020_f08e518f", 
"title": "ON THE FAMOUS MYCENAEAN SIGNET RING, CMS I, 179, OF THE TIRYNS TREASURE", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/4389733/ON_THE_FAMOUS_MYCENAEAN_SIGNET_RING_CMS_I_179_OF_THE_TIRYNS_TREASURE", 
"source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003432757158_88d73165", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Available AI‑derived summaries report that the Mycenaean signet ring CMS I, 179 (Tiryns Treasure) measures 
approximately 3.5 by 5.7 cm, has an annulus diameter of about 2.2 cm, weighs around 82.95 grams, and is described 
there as the largest known Mycenaean signet ring, allegedly unearthed in December 1915 by Apostolos Arvanitopoulos.",
"source_ids": ["20260719003338997020_f08e518f"], "citations": [1], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003338997020_f08e518f", "title": "ON THE FAMOUS MYCENAEAN SIGNET RING, CMS I, 179, OF THE TIRYNS TREASURE", 
"url": "https://www.academia.edu/4389733/ON_THE_FAMOUS_MYCENAEAN_SIGNET_RING_CMS_I_179_OF_THE_TIRYNS_TREASURE", 
"source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003432805297_b1c525c0", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"The Papathanassiou study is presented on Academia.edu as authored by Dr. Kyriakos Papathanassiou, an alumnus of New 
York University working in the history of art and archaeology (including Ancient Egypt and Ancient Greece), but this 
profile information is self‑reported on the hosting platform rather than vetted by a scholarly publisher.", 
"source_ids": ["20260719003338997020_f08e518f"], "citations": [1], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003338997020_f08e518f", "title": "ON THE FAMOUS MYCENAEAN SIGNET RING, CMS I, 179, OF THE TIRYNS TREASURE", 
"url": "https://www.academia.edu/4389733/ON_THE_FAMOUS_MYCENAEAN_SIGNET_RING_CMS_I_179_OF_THE_TIRYNS_TREASURE", 
"source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}]}], "offset": 0, "limit": 50, "total": 32, "next_offset": null}
