"https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_RING_OF_NESTOR_", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003934555021_7711b740", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“This paper 
presents an analysis of the iconography surrounding the 'Ring of Nestor' in the context of Aegean forgeries and 
authenticity debates. By revisiting key iconographic features and comparisons, the paper aims to contribute to the 
ongoing discourse regarding the ring's significance in Minoan and Mycenaean art. Observations draw upon historical 
scholarship and recent discoveries to question and affirm previous conclusions related to the ring's motifs and their
implications.”", "source_ids": ["20260719003933769555_a2004ad2"], "citations": [6], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003933769555_a2004ad2", "title": "SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING OF NESTOR’", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_RING_OF_NESTOR_", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719003934567933_12441a56", "type": "Evidence", "text": "“1.   The text
explores the authenticity of the 'Ring of Nestor' through iconographic analysis.\n2.   Contradictions in technical 
analysis highlight the importance of iconography in Aegean studies.\n3.   The connection between incurved altars and 
the ring's depiction supports its authenticity.\n4.   The ring's iconography may reflect cultural practices from the 
1920s.\n5.   Generations of scholars have debated the genuineness of both Nestor's and Minos' rings.”", "source_ids":
["20260719003933769555_a2004ad2"], "citations": [6], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003933769555_a2004ad2", 
"title": "SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING OF NESTOR’", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_RING_OF_NESTOR_", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004114649038_35628153", "type": "Claim", "text": "Theodore 
Eliopoulos authored the article “Some Observations on the Iconography of the ‘Ring of Nestor’,” published in volume 
XCI (2013) of Annuario della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente, pages 209–228, which 
examines the iconography and authenticity of the so‑called Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277) in the context of Aegean 
forgery debates.", "source_ids": ["20260719003933749168_bc366e4a", "20260719003933769555_a2004ad2"], "citations": [4,
6], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003933749168_bc366e4a", "title": "(2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of 
Nestor and the quest for authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean 
Iconography, Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223-252.", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A.G._Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F._Bl
akolmer_ed._Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_AEGIS_2020_pp._223-252", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}, {"source_id": "20260719003933769555_a2004ad2", "title": "SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE 
ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING OF NESTOR’", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_RING_OF_NESTOR_", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004114702120_1c2852e9", "type": "Claim", "text": "Eliopoulos’s 
study concludes that the Ring of Nestor (and related Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings, including the so‑called Ring of 
Minos) is authentic, overturning earlier 20th‑century scholarly opinions that had classified such rings as modern 
forgeries, and this reassessment is supported by recent discoveries of comparable iconographic material and by 
detailed study of Late Bronze Age signet‑ring techniques.", "source_ids": ["20260719003933749168_bc366e4a", 
"20260719003933769555_a2004ad2"], "citations": [4, 6], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003933749168_bc366e4a", 
"title": "(2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) 
Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography, Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223-252.", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A.G._Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F._Bl
akolmer_ed._Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_AEGIS_2020_pp._223-252", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}, {"source_id": "20260719003933769555_a2004ad2", "title": "SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE 
ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING OF NESTOR’", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_RING_OF_NESTOR_", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004114744117_b641ce24", "type": "Claim", "text": "In Eliopoulos’s 
interpretation, the key pictorial unit on the Ring of Nestor—previously described by Arthur Evans as a ‘watch‑dog’ or
‘dragon’ guarding a world‑tree—is better identified as a scorpion, an apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean 
iconography, which is depicted killing the so‑called ‘Minoan Dragon’ and thereby provides a new explanation for the 
ring’s imagery.", "source_ids": ["20260719003933749168_bc366e4a"], "citations": [4], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003933749168_bc366e4a", "title": "(2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for 
authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography, Aegis 18 (2020), 
pp. 223-252.", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A.G._Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F._Bl
akolmer_ed._Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_AEGIS_2020_pp._223-252", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004114773892_e5db951a", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of 
Nestor is dated, in Eliopoulos’s discussion, to the early part of the Late Bronze Age (approximately the 16th–15th 
centuries BC), situating it within the broader corpus of large Aegean signet rings of that period.", "source_ids": 
["20260719003933749168_bc366e4a"], "citations": [4], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003933749168_bc366e4a", 
"title": "(2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) 
Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography, Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223-252.", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A.G._Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F._Bl
akolmer_ed._Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_AEGIS_2020_pp._223-252", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004114791173_f83f41a6", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of 
Nestor’s engravings include a complex scene around a ‘world tree’ whose branches bear an unusually large number of 
figures for Aegean glyptic art, and the scene has been variously interpreted as a ritual or sacrificial context with 
strong religious and symbolic themes relating to divinity, nature, cyclicality, and possibly concepts of eternity.", 
"source_ids": ["20260719003338997020_f08e518f", "20260719003933729169_8600543a", "20260719003933749168_bc366e4a"], 
"citations": [1, 3, 4], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003338997020_f08e518f", "title": "ON THE FAMOUS MYCENAEAN 
SIGNET RING, CMS I, 179, OF THE TIRYNS TREASURE", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/4389733/ON_THE_FAMOUS_MYCENAEAN_SIGNET_RING_CMS_I_179_OF_THE_TIRYNS_TREASURE", 
"source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}, {"source_id": "20260719003933729169_8600543a", "title": "Rare 
3,500-Year-Old Mycenaean Gold Signet Ring Discovered in Pylos | IttiHaas Chronicle", "url": 
