akolmer_ed._Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_AEGIS_2020_pp._223-252", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004114834402_adaeec71", "type": "Claim", "text": "Iconographic 
interpretations of the Ring of Nestor include the proposal that its main scene features a female deity (possibly 
identifiable with Demeter) accompanied by symbols of fertility, such as grain and an eagle associated with Zeus, and 
that these motifs reflect spiritual themes characteristic of the LH II period.", "source_ids": 
["20260719003338997020_f08e518f"], "citations": [1], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003338997020_f08e518f", 
"title": "ON THE FAMOUS MYCENAEAN SIGNET RING, CMS I, 179, OF THE TIRYNS TREASURE", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/4389733/ON_THE_FAMOUS_MYCENAEAN_SIGNET_RING_CMS_I_179_OF_THE_TIRYNS_TREASURE", 
"source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004114853289_f7825797", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Some interpretations of the Ring of Nestor’s symbolism explicitly acknowledge that our limited knowledge of the 
archaeology, mythology, and historical context of the Middle Helladic and LH II periods constrains certainty, and 
therefore require cautious and imaginative engagement when proposing meanings for the ring’s motifs.", "source_ids": 
["20260719003338997020_f08e518f"], "citations": [1], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003338997020_f08e518f", 
"title": "ON THE FAMOUS MYCENAEAN SIGNET RING, CMS I, 179, OF THE TIRYNS TREASURE", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/4389733/ON_THE_FAMOUS_MYCENAEAN_SIGNET_RING_CMS_I_179_OF_THE_TIRYNS_TREASURE", 
"source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004114873321_a4b48dcd", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Within Mycenaean cultural interpretation, the Ring of Nestor (CMS I, 179) has been characterized as a κειμήλιο 
(heirloom), implying that it functioned as a valued ancestral object connected to notions of ancestral respect and 
cultural identity.", "source_ids": ["20260719003338997020_f08e518f"], "citations": [1], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003338997020_f08e518f", "title": "ON THE FAMOUS MYCENAEAN SIGNET RING, CMS I, 179, OF THE TIRYNS TREASURE", 
"url": "https://www.academia.edu/4389733/ON_THE_FAMOUS_MYCENAEAN_SIGNET_RING_CMS_I_179_OF_THE_TIRYNS_TREASURE", 
"source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004114893392_afc31088", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"The Mycenaean signet ring CMS I, 179 (identified in these summaries with the Ring of Nestor) was unearthed in 
December 1915 by Apostolos Arvanitopoulos and has been described as the largest known Mycenaean signet ring, 
measuring approximately 3.5 by 5.7 cm with an annulus diameter of about 2.2 cm and weighing around 82.95 grams, 
though some summaries alternatively report dimensions of 3.29 by 2.18 cm and rank it as the fifth largest Aegean 
signet ring.", "source_ids": ["20260719003338997020_f08e518f", "20260719003933749168_bc366e4a"], "citations": [1, 4],
