“Some Observations on the Iconography of the ‘Ring of Nestor’,” published in volume XCI (2013) of Annuario della 
Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente, pages 209–228, which analyzes the ring’s 
iconography in the context of Aegean forgery and authenticity debates.", "source_ids": 
["20260719003933769555_a2004ad2"], "citations": [6], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003933769555_a2004ad2", 
"title": "SOME OBSERVATIONS ON THE ICONOGRAPHY OF THE ‘RING OF NESTOR’", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_RING_OF_NESTOR_", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004840739013_16c22d95", "type": "Claim", "text": "The key pictorial
unit on the Ring of Nestor discussed by Eliopoulos is the motif previously termed by Evans as a ‘watch‑dog’ or 
‘dragon’ guarding a ‘tree of the world’, whose branches contain an unusually populous scene within Aegean glyptic 
art; Eliopoulos reinterprets this motif as a scorpion, an important apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean 
iconography, which ‘kills’ the so‑called Minoan dragon and thereby provides a new explanation of the ring’s 
imagery.", "source_ids": ["20260719003933749168_bc366e4a"], "citations": [4], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003933749168_bc366e4a", "title": "(2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for 
authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography, Aegis 18 (2020), 
pp. 223-252.", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A.G._Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F._Bl
akolmer_ed._Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_AEGIS_2020_pp._223-252", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004840776413_cac08a78", "type": "Claim", "text": "Recent 
reassessment of the Ring of Nestor’s authenticity has been reinforced by newly found Late Bronze Age (16th–15th 
century BC) artworks with comparable iconographic subjects (such as wall paintings, gold rings, figurines, and 
ivories) and by more detailed study of the techniques and craftsmanship of large signet rings from the early Late 
Bronze Age.", "source_ids": ["20260719003933749168_bc366e4a"], "citations": [4], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003933749168_bc366e4a", "title": "(2020) A. G. Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for 
authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) Current Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography, Aegis 18 (2020), 
pp. 223-252.", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A.G._Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F._Bl
akolmer_ed._Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_AEGIS_2020_pp._223-252", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004840796356_965bd5fc", "type": "Claim", "text": "Some descriptions
classify the Ring of Nestor as a Late Minoan‑period gold signet ring, approximately dated to c. 1700–1450 BC, and 
refer to it as a ‘Minoan Kakovatos signet ring’, though this attribution currently rests on secondary, user‑generated
sources.", "source_ids": ["20260719003933759826_2aed0dcc"], "citations": [5], "sources": [{"source_id": 
"20260719003933759826_2aed0dcc", "title": "The 'Ring of Nestor' signet ring. Late Minoan Period, c.1700-1450 BC 
(gold) Minoan Kakovatos signet ring; the so-called … | Gold intaglio, Minoan gold, Minoan ring", "url": 
"https://www.pinterest.com/pin/378795018652886171", "source_type": "other", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": 
"20260719004840826757_332db275", "type": "Claim", "text": "Popular and AI‑generated summaries state that the Ring of 
Nestor has dimensions around 3.29 × 2.18 cm and that it ranks among the largest Aegean signet rings (sometimes 
described as the fifth largest), but these measurements are not consistently reported and may conflict with other 
size data.", "source_ids": ["20260719003338997020_f08e518f", "20260719003933749168_bc366e4a"], "citations": [1, 4], 
"sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003338997020_f08e518f", "title": "ON THE FAMOUS MYCENAEAN SIGNET RING, CMS I, 179,
OF THE TIRYNS TREASURE", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/4389733/ON_THE_FAMOUS_MYCENAEAN_SIGNET_RING_CMS_I_179_OF_THE_TIRYNS_TREASURE", 
"source_type": "paper", "published_at": null}, {"source_id": "20260719003933749168_bc366e4a", "title": "(2020) A. G. 
Vlachopoulos, “The Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity”, in F. Blakolmer (ed.) Current Approaches and New 
Perspectives in Aegean Iconography, Aegis 18 (2020), pp. 223-252.", "url": 
"https://www.academia.edu/43416584/_2020_A.G._Vlachopoulos_The_Ring_of_Nestor_and_the_quest_for_authenticity_in_F._Bl
akolmer_ed._Current_Approaches_and_New_Perspectives_in_Aegean_Iconography_AEGIS_2020_pp._223-252", "source_type": 
"paper", "published_at": null}]}, {"id": "20260719004840964790_1b889493", "type": "Claim", "text": "Interpretations 
of the Ring of Nestor’s imagery emphasize its ritual and cosmological themes, including depictions of a female deity,
fertility symbols such as grain and an eagle of Zeus, four seasonal symbols, and combined sun‑and‑moon motifs that 
together suggest cycles of nature and concepts of eternity, but these remain speculative iconographic readings 
constrained by limited knowledge of LH II and MH contexts.", "source_ids": ["20260719003338997020_f08e518f"], 
"citations": [1], "sources": [{"source_id": "20260719003338997020_f08e518f", "title": "ON THE FAMOUS MYCENAEAN SIGNET
