that had classified the Ring of Nestor and related Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings, such as the so‑called Ring of Minos, 
as forgeries.[4][6] These studies conclude that the Ring of Nestor should be regarded as authentic, and they support 
this position by highlighting new Late Bronze Age finds with comparable iconographic subjects and by analysing the 
techniques and craftsmanship of large signet rings from the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age.[4][6] In 
particular, they emphasize the ring’s alignment with securely excavated assemblages such as the Griffin Warrior tomb 
at Pylos, whose gold signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual scenes demonstrate that complex, Minoan‑style glyptic 
imagery circulated in Mycenaean elite contexts during the same broad period.[7][8]\n\nDespite these reassessments, 
recent work also cautions that expecting new material alone to provide a definitive key to the authenticity of gemmae
dubitandae like the Ring of Nestor is unrealistic.[2] Scholarly opinion continues to reflect a spectrum of views, 
ranging from cautious acceptance rooted in iconographic and technical comparison to persistent reservations grounded 
in the ring’s unprovenanced status. The object therefore remains central to discussions of how, and to what extent, 
unprovenanced artifacts can be integrated into reconstructions of Aegean Bronze Age art and religion.[2][4][6]', 
