'disclosed_conflict_ids': [], 'section_type': 'body'}, {'title': 'Reception and significance', 'content': 'The Ring 
of Nestor occupies a prominent place in modern studies of Aegean Bronze Age iconography, religion and archaeological 
method. Arthur Evans’s early interpretation of the ring as a depiction of a Minoan Elysian afterlife contributed to 
shaping influential narratives about Minoan religion and eschatology, and more broadly about the evolution of Aegean 
Bronze Age civilizations.[2] Subsequent historiographical analyses note that Evans and other pioneers of Cretan 
archaeology established terminological frameworks and chronological benchmarks that structured the discipline for 
decades, and that the Ring of Nestor formed part of this formative constellation of images and artifacts used to 
define Minoan and Mycenaean religious experience.[2]\n\nLater scholarship has approached the ring both as a 
significant piece of religious iconography and as a methodological problem. Iconographic studies by Theodore 
Eliopoulos and A. G. Vlachopoulos treat the ring as an important example of early Late Bronze Age Creto‑Mycenaean 
gold glyptic, whose densely populated scene around a world tree offers insight into the representation of divine 
power, protective symbols and ritual activity.[4][6] These authors argue that the ring’s imagery resonates with 
themes visible in other Late Bronze Age artworks, including wall paintings, figurines, ivories and gold rings, and 
that it should be integrated into broader discussions of Aegean religious and mythological symbolism.[4]\n\nAt the 
same time, the Ring of Nestor’s contested provenance and history of assessment make it a paradigmatic case study in 
debates over authenticity, forgery and the treatment of unprovenanced objects. Discussions of gemmae dubitandae 
employ the ring as a focal example when examining how uncertain artifacts can be both used and questioned within 
archaeological narratives, and how relational archaeology perspectives foreground the interactions of forgers, 
collectors, excavators and scholars in shaping what counts as evidence.[2] Comparative work that juxtaposes the Ring 
of Nestor with securely provenanced finds, such as the gold signet rings from the Griffin Warrior tomb near the 
Palace of Nestor, underscores its value as a point of reference for understanding the circulation and adaptation of 
Minoan ritual imagery in Mycenaean elite contexts.[4][7][8]\n\nBecause of this dual role—as both a rich source of 
iconographic material and an object of enduring controversy—the Ring of Nestor remains a touchstone in discussions 
about methodological rigor, the limits of interpretation and the ethical and epistemological challenges posed by 
unprovenanced antiquities. Scholars continue to draw on the ring when exploring how stylistic, iconographic and 
technical analyses can be balanced against caution in the use of objects whose archaeological histories are 
incomplete, and when reflecting on the ways in which disciplinary traditions and changing theoretical approaches 
shape the reading of Aegean Bronze Age imagery.[2][4][6]', 'source_ids': ['20260719003339049197_43b4b972', 
